export const body = "<p>Chapter 07: Navigation Aids</p>\n\n<h2>What’s the title about?</h2>\n\n<p><em>Navigation</em>, in this context, simply means wayfinding, and <em>aids</em> means support. By that logic, navigation aids mean tools that support wayfinding. Before we dive into the specific tools, let’s warm up with the concept of breadcrumbs.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d37104cac4a60320f00e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d37104cac4a60320f00e.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Breadcrumbs</h2>\n\n<p>Breadcrumbs are visual elements—props, textures, lights—that are placed in a trail to lead the player through an environment, one piece at a time.</p>\n\n<p>Throughout this chapter, we will look at different tools that can be used like breadcrumbs.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4eb24936990c91f4546a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4eb24936990c91f4546a.webp\" width=\"877\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:525px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>EXAMPLE — Breadcumbs</h3>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/594e9447b158e686da98.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/594e9447b158e686da98.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bcf8c88293cec7eeb723.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bcf8c88293cec7eeb723.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"616\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d0aed8c269fa1ca3a265.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d0aed8c269fa1ca3a265.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a3c770452f41e0ef4cb5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a3c770452f41e0ef4cb5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"583\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Here, we see two examples of using textures to guide the player. In the left picture, the scratches on the wall lead the player to the banners. In the right picture, the tire tracks hint at which direction to take.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f0562c4cc65fb01f32c7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f0562c4cc65fb01f32c7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"284\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>You can quickly sprinkle some breadcrumbs in the blockout, then iterate on them later as you polish the level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a550bed1bc5c533c9042.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a550bed1bc5c533c9042.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"304\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Affordances</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>An affordance is an object that affords an action. For example, a door handle affords pulling while metal plate affords pushing. With affordances, designers can convey the usage to users without direct guidance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0d56fe67c7147b2f12bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0d56fe67c7147b2f12bd.webp\" width=\"792\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:474px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>How to make one?</h3>\n\n<p>As a level artist, your main goal with affordances is to sculpt distinct and consistent shapes so that they can convey interactions to the player at a glance. Your affordances, of course, have to follow the design metrics.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dbf1c6d92ade2461c74c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dbf1c6d92ade2461c74c.webp\" width=\"856\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:512px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Affordances</strong></p>\n\n<p>In this example, the cart affords a higher jump like a ramp. It is also positioned slightly upward, pointing at the rope above.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/be9dfe685c17bd9107e6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/be9dfe685c17bd9107e6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Wooden beams are the most common affordances in <em>Darksiders</em><em> 2</em>.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/61a79c5b7457a2f5cf1a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/61a79c5b7457a2f5cf1a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"370\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Portal 2</em> allows the player to open interdimensional gates to solve puzzles. These orange and blue gates are the core mechanic, and thus used frequently thorough the game. And to make this mechanic widely viable, most surfaces in the game (walls, floors, ceilings) are flat. Flat surfaces afford portal opening.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5c7ce6219333a334f48b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5c7ce6219333a334f48b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Prince of Persia </em>keeps most vertical surfaces flat (or relatively flat) so that the player can run on or bounce off of them. In this case, walls afford running and bouncing.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e36a7dd33e5d24a1875c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e36a7dd33e5d24a1875c.webp\" width=\"469\" height=\"264\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2da00b8685a9e87141eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2da00b8685a9e87141eb.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"379\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p>The first mission takes place in a literal mental warzone (it’s all inside a person’s head). Therefore, many affordances are adapted to military context. From left to right, we have: airships used as moving platforms, gun barrels as swing poles, and landing gear as oscillating swing poles.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/552ba6fdd2bcc5e021f7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/552ba6fdd2bcc5e021f7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"288\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The first mission takes place in a literal mental warzone (it’s all inside a person’s head). Therefore, many affordances are adapted to military context. From left to right, we have: airships used as moving platforms, gun barrels as swing poles, and landing gear as oscillating swing poles.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/552ba6fdd2bcc5e021f7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/552ba6fdd2bcc5e021f7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"288\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Cover is a common form of affordances in shooting games. Usually, cover objects have rectangular shapes because they look sturdy and reliable. While some games use a consistent type of objects for cover, others change theirs based on the environment. Regardless, gameplay-bound objects such as cover must always follow design metrics.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3152a780c834b68147a7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3152a780c834b68147a7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"344\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Bad</strong><strong> affordances</strong></p>\n\n<p>In this example from <em>Ninja </em><em>Gaiden</em><em> (2004)</em>, the metal bar that leads you to the other side of the carrier is barely visible.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e388ad770e66554f057d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e388ad770e66554f057d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Here we have the original <em>Half-Life</em> (1996) and its remake version called <em>Black Mesa</em>. Although <em>Black Mesa </em>has much better graphics, it falls short in terms of clarity. The 1996 game makes it clear that the pipes are platforms. But in the new game, the pipes blend in with the surroundings, making it harder to discern. Besides, using a realistic tubular shape unfornately makes them look less like platforms.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0fcff8715bb843f68d2d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0fcff8715bb843f68d2d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The stair in <em>Resident Evil Village</em> blends into the environment, barely readable. The stair in <em>Resident Evil 3 Remake </em>with yellow paint is a lot more visible (even without the torch).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/530df6d6136511f197e8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/530df6d6136511f197e8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Below is <em>Bleak Faith Forsaken</em>, an indie action role-playing game. The affordances (ladder and grab-able edge) don’t have enough contrast against the environment, so they aren’t very visible. This example is quite unfair because the screenshots are taken from an early build of the game. But it is still a good reminder for us to make affordances more readable.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d5ef211d8cabc9a7ddc6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d5ef211d8cabc9a7ddc6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In this example from <em>Borderlands 3</em>, the ladder is barely visible due to the dark environment, making the area seem like a dead end. The dark color in conjunction with the “players rarely look up” fact make it hard to identify where the path is in this place.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cc4b4042453daea9b6a2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cc4b4042453daea9b6a2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Deny affordances</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>In Chapter 1, we briefly talked about how games block off non-playable areas with props to remove guesswork. When you see that happens, it is deny affordances in action.  Whereas affordances enable an action, deny affordances disable an action.</p>\n\n<p>When designing deny affordances, it helps to think within the context of your game world, instead of conforming to real-life logic.</p>\n\n<p>As you can see in the opposite pictures, if these were real-life situations, the wooden beams and planks wouldn’t prevent you from accessing the area. But in these games, they work fine.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/14c10ed14fd9f58ebb6d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/14c10ed14fd9f58ebb6d.webp\" width=\"870\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:520px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>A humble suggestion</h3>\n\n<p>If it helps, you can think of deny affordances like real-life trespassing warnings, such as construction barricade or traffic cones. While you are physically capable of vaulting over them, you wouldn’t do it, right?</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, your deny affordances don’t have to be absolute obstruction. As long as they convince the player to act within the constraints of the gameplay, they’re good enough.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2c7022062731a9008616.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2c7022062731a9008616.webp\" width=\"929\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:555px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1fe0645773d6320b9a7f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1fe0645773d6320b9a7f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"878\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>For indoor environments, furniture will be your best friend for blockage.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eaac5d79ae4f890bbf33.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eaac5d79ae4f890bbf33.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Destruction makes for great blockers.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7d7c445dd7e2296fbc65.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7d7c445dd7e2296fbc65.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"347\" style=\"--source-width:170px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f596a01910b74fe67e26.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f596a01910b74fe67e26.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"282\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p>Using spiky shapes for blockers is one common way to deny access without covering the entire opening. Admittedly, it doesn’t look realistic at all, but it works.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/439f5913ae6a2892a4d5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/439f5913ae6a2892a4d5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In both of these examples, the deny affordances break up the flat pattern of the objects, making them look uninteractable.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f465eacc86172bde84fd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f465eacc86172bde84fd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"318\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>In this sequence from <em>The Last of Us 2</em>, the player is ambushed by an infected enemy as soon as they walked through the broken door. In order to lock the player in this room, the game is scripted to collapse some wooden beams to block the door.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/99f90ee3be38f8ee79cb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/99f90ee3be38f8ee79cb.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"347\" style=\"--source-width:125px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d29eb000692b18f9026c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d29eb000692b18f9026c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Signifiers</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>A signifier is a label that explains the usage of an object. Let’s go back to the door example. Here, the metal plate is an affordance, but the word PUSH is a signifier.</p>\n\n<p>In video games, signifiers don’t always have to be words. For example, <em>Sekiro</em><em>: Shadows Die Twice</em> uses white paint to denote grab-able ledges.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1796992a6c552bfc435a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1796992a6c552bfc435a.webp\" width=\"851\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:509px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>What is the difference with an affordance?</h3>\n\n<p>Here is an example to help you distinguish affordances and signifiers more clearly. In <em>Ratchet and Clank: Rift Apart</em>, the player character can perform wall running on certain hovering structures.</p>\n\n<p>Within these structures, the panels are affordances because they afford wall running. But the arrows flashing on their surfaces are signifiers because they signify the direction to which your character is running.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fb6b44b88ff46aa35d77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fb6b44b88ff46aa35d77.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:532px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Why is it important?</h3>\n\n<p>Let’s examine the importance of signifiers. Climbing is a central mechanic in the <em>Uncharted </em>series; therefore, climbable objects are key to the navigation of a level.</p>\n\n<p>In the top picture from <em>Uncharted 1</em>, there are climbable ledges scattered around the space, but you cannot immediately see them. You either have to get close or squint your eyes from afar to find the direction.</p>\n\n<p><em>Uncharted 2</em> improved on this aspect by signifying their affordances with colors. As you can see in the opposite picture, the climable objects are painted red.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d71291feb7eee0913644.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d71291feb7eee0913644.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Signifiers</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f808df14452dcf05f448.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f808df14452dcf05f448.webp\" width=\"828\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><em>The Last of Us </em>uses yellow for many signifiers throughout.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6038e282749df4144f04.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6038e282749df4144f04.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"337\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Taking place in a fantasy universe, <em>God of War (2018)</em> blends the yellow signifiers into the game world by giving them glyph-like designs or turning them into a part of a bigger structure.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/384d0648d65cea3c8f7e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/384d0648d65cea3c8f7e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Borderlands 3 </em>also uses yellow for its signifiers. Note how the signifiers are contextualized in the right picture: the one below is paint, and the one above is a chevron traffic sign.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d4ba098a06657ac397a5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d4ba098a06657ac397a5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>I promise this is the last slide with yellow signifiers.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a70d03defd2dd0ce3420.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a70d03defd2dd0ce3420.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Leaving yellow behind, let’s try white instead.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/983d09083c28f0872828.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/983d09083c28f0872828.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"316\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>It’s important to keep your signifiers looking consistent with their surroundings. This aspect varies from game to game (some games keep their signifiers unchanged throughout), but it’s something you should keep in mind.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/847cdad6b2284e192f0d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/847cdad6b2284e192f0d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>One game that famously keeps all signifiers looking consistent throughout is <em>Mirror’s Edge</em>. Once the player activates Runner’s Vision, every affordance in the game will appear red.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/51c17be67ebf52ffe6a9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/51c17be67ebf52ffe6a9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 44\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Landmarks</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>A landmark is (often) a building or place that is easily recognized from afar, and thus can be used to judge distance and position. And this is also how game designers view landmarks. They add these huge structures in their levels in order to tell the player where they should be headed, or where they currently are.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/76120c58748ed2096f6b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/76120c58748ed2096f6b.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 45\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Why do we need one?</h3>\n\n<p><strong>In linear games</strong>: a landmark allows the player to gauge their distance relative to the objective. For example, the Citadel in <em>Half-Life 2</em> is the central landmark that appears every once in a while informs the player of his progress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>In open-world games</strong>: a landmark doubles as an attraction that invites the player to participate in a side activity. For example, <em>Breath of the Wild</em> has plenty of landmarks of various sizes and shapes sprinkled across its map to encourage exploration.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c2456dfd0e3366c85a95.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c2456dfd0e3366c85a95.webp\" width=\"884\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:529px\" alt=\"Illustration 46\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Landmarks</strong><strong><br></strong><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6ab5acd54ab1eae367ca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6ab5acd54ab1eae367ca.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"416\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3dad115e1252a77de4f6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3dad115e1252a77de4f6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"579\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/45eb20ea6f1e60423546.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/45eb20ea6f1e60423546.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c6f484615b68127b9d26.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c6f484615b68127b9d26.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/53e476dfaad5b2d76c91.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/53e476dfaad5b2d76c91.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9f281465ac4b150ea1fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9f281465ac4b150ea1fa.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Grounded </em>tells the story of four teenagers who get shrunk to miniature size and have to survive in a backyard. Resource gathering is a huge part of the game since one of its themes is survival. Therefore, having landmarks to dole out different areas is a necessity. Playing to its whimsical premise, <em>Grounded</em> turns day-to-day objects—a hairpin, a mini shovel, a thrown away toy—into large points of interest.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8b2ebdcac7270fcbc55e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8b2ebdcac7270fcbc55e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5885196a33fa808bb789.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5885196a33fa808bb789.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"399\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eddc416d46384c4b958c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eddc416d46384c4b958c.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:532px\" alt=\"Illustration 55\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> </strong><strong>– Lighting guidance</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Skywells, natural or man-made, are a great tool to pour beams of light into a confined space.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6d5f6fd2875ce1bee895.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6d5f6fd2875ce1bee895.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"301\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 56\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0c6c1b4d62f65ad16b08.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0c6c1b4d62f65ad16b08.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"357\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 57\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/043dadd961d31e18e7e1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/043dadd961d31e18e7e1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"340\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 58\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/951994448dd1be55b34a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/951994448dd1be55b34a.webp\" width=\"828\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 59\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The candles are placed to highlight the item box.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4584eebf756c45d50cc9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4584eebf756c45d50cc9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 60\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b4636766f397b17c0b39.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b4636766f397b17c0b39.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"644\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 61\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b9d07b9cab05b28b27f8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b9d07b9cab05b28b27f8.webp\" width=\"1104\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 62\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Warm-cool contrast (or different-colored lights) can help indicate key interactions in your environments.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5286cc50269005b297d9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5286cc50269005b297d9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 63\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Warm-cool contrast (or different-colored lights) can help indicate key interactions in your environments.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a64f91cd5fae582b0e38.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a64f91cd5fae582b0e38.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 64\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Motion</h2>\n\n<p>In a static environment, anything that moves will attract attention (contrast, remember?). Motion can come from a flapping doors, a flickering light, or a flock of birds. There are so many ways to integrate movement into your environment, so the main difficulty is communicating with whoever does the scripting for your level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/536e5c79fe8596ad1848.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/536e5c79fe8596ad1848.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:480px\" alt=\"Illustration 65\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ebf6134c0a1d776b248.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ebf6134c0a1d776b248.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:480px\" alt=\"Illustration 66\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>NPC (non-playable character)</h2>\n\n<p>Also related to scripting is the use of non-playable characters for guidance. Sometimes, it can be a mission where the player is led by his companion; other times, it is a mission where the player has to stalk a target (like the tailing missions in <em>Assassin’s Creed</em>).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4c1e2b44b5ea12a006b9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4c1e2b44b5ea12a006b9.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 67\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>NPC</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ca213be8ae91138b3fb5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ca213be8ae91138b3fb5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"613\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 68\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b5584ad1758fa7884a26.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b5584ad1758fa7884a26.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"862\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 69\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/088f0f31e841b0879c5a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/088f0f31e841b0879c5a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"616\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 70\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Openings</h2>\n\n<p>Openings, like doors or cracked walls, are also good at attracting attention. They either offer refuge space or stir the player’s curiosity, inviting them to explore.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9551bb2fc4858f4632b2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9551bb2fc4858f4632b2.webp\" width=\"885\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:529px\" alt=\"Illustration 71\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Openings</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8c0aa10d4bc851f0dbff.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8c0aa10d4bc851f0dbff.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 72\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ec9e3dcf932fa18ddd38.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ec9e3dcf932fa18ddd38.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 73\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bc2a329d28233dd2efd6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bc2a329d28233dd2efd6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 74\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bf954d5847842b9f47ac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bf954d5847842b9f47ac.webp\" width=\"1036\" height=\"585\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 75\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Dealing with corners</h2>\n\n<p>In 3D, it’s harder to know which way to turn at a 3-way intersection. To remove guesswork, you can add some props to nudge the player toward the intended direction.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0bbb0d34db9c0532e734.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0bbb0d34db9c0532e734.webp\" width=\"830\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:496px\" alt=\"Illustration 76\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Dealing with corners</strong></p>\n\n<p>Or, you can make a curved corner.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5b395ed291116624d8f6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5b395ed291116624d8f6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 77\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Adding light to attract the player to the right corner is also a viable strategy. In the left picture below, the yellow pipes lead our eyes to the left corner where light is streaming in.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/84c859deef562b9b55a1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/84c859deef562b9b55a1.webp\" width=\"397\" height=\"224\" style=\"--source-width:130px\" alt=\"Illustration 78\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/152a440be3e625e5ccff.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/152a440be3e625e5ccff.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 79\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Case study: </strong><em><strong>Star War Jedi Fallen Order</strong></em></p>\n\n<p>Now that we have learned all navigation aids, it’s time to see how they work in practice. Here, we will look at the opening of<em> Jedi Fallen Order</em>. This introductory level takes place on Bracca, a junkyard planet. The story begins when Kal, the player character, meets with Prauf, an NPC, for a mission.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3ef2cce8b7721d892aca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3ef2cce8b7721d892aca.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 80\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>After a short scene, it’s established that Prauf will be the guide on this mission. So, this is NPC guidance. As the player follows their alien colleague, they learn how to jump and climb over obstacles. The basic controls are quickly taught.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c6e1f0c3867eb9007107.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c6e1f0c3867eb9007107.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 81\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>After climbing a couple of times, the player will likely pickup the visual language for the affordances. They are rectangular edges signified by a red paint.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/48a7477b91e6a73cb311.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/48a7477b91e6a73cb311.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 82\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>After another short cutscene, Prauf stopped accompanying the player, so they have to find the way themselves. In the left picture, you can see the open door angled toward the narrow game; there is also an arrow sign on it. Opposite to the door is a low lamp, which illuminate the signifier. The red metal sheet on the ground also points at the gap to help. Similarly, in the right picture, props are placed to nudge the player toward the right side. The small lights there also help attract their attention.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3c71a29b529d0776e18f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3c71a29b529d0776e18f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"318\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 83\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Turning to the right, the player will slide down a steep surface, which introduces them to a new mechanic. Steep, smooth surfaces afford sliding. At the end of the slide, they’ll meet a droid which points them to the next location. Again, this is NPC guidance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/159370b8779f77778f67.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/159370b8779f77778f67.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 84\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The game continues to use red props and NPC guidance to lead the player from place to place. The next traversal mechanic is climbing which is also informed by a distinct red surface. This grid pattern affords climbing throughout the game, but different areas will have different colors for it.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/19dd328f289c9303af12.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/19dd328f289c9303af12.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"325\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 85\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Now, the player has to balance himself on a metal beam, which is an affordance doubling as a leading line. Once the player got to the other end, they’ll see a droid shinning light and rotating in place, which is a combination of motion and light. In addition, the metal planks on the ground serve as breadcrumbs, pointing at the small gap.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a0d06a866b8424d939f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a0d06a866b8424d939f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"319\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 86\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The final traversal mechanic the player learns in this section is rope swinging. Admittedly, the rope looks a bit too dark sometimes, but stands out from the environment more often than not. (Note in the left picture that the affordances are placed like a breadcrumb trail.)</p>\n\n<p>And that’s the end of our case study. Time for the final chapter!</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dca1cdd292ae9f27ef5b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dca1cdd292ae9f27ef5b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 87\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
